[2026-11-19T11:53:07.029Z] [DEBUG] [client] MCP client request body, backend=safeoutputs, body={"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"create_pull_request","arguments":{"title":"Add the Deepal E07, Mitsubishi ASX VR-e LS, MG U9 EV and Xpeng X9","branch":"catalogue/2026-11-19-new-evs","body":"Adds six EVs that are now on sale or priced in Australia. One is from a make that's new to the file (Mitsubishi). No existing entries are changed, and `validate.mjs` passes (232 vehicles, every id kept).\n\n| Entry (new) | Battery / usable | WLTP | DC / AC | Charge claim | Source and exact figures |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `deepal-e07-rwd-2025` (suv) | 89.98 kWh / 86 est. | 550 km | 240 / 7 kW | 30–80% 15 min | [deepal.com.au E07](https://www.deepal.com.au/vehicles/deepal/e07/), checked 19 Nov 2026: \"89.98kWh NMC battery\", \"550kms\", \"240kW\", \"7kW AC charging\", \"DC Charge Time 30%-80%: 15 minutes\" |\n| `deepal-e07-awd-performance-2025` (suv) | 89.98 kWh / 86 est. | 510 km | 240 / 7 kW | 30–80% 15 min | Same page: \"510kms\", \"240kW\" |\n| `mitsubishi-asx-vr-e-ls-2026` (suv, **new make**) | 57.7 kWh / 55 est. | 403 km | 134 / 6.6 kW | 10–80% about 30 min | [GoAuto, 19 Nov 2026](https://www.goauto.com.au/new-models/mitsubishi/asx/vre/2026-11-19/101115.html): \"LS has the furthest driving range at 403km on the WLTP cycle\", \"134kW CCS2 DC charging that allows 10 to 80 per cent recharges in approximately half an hour\". Pricing announced by Mitsubishi ($46,740 + ORCs) per [The Driven, 19 Nov 2026](https://thedriven.io/2026/11/19/mitsubishi-asx-vr-e-electric-suv-to-launch-from-45990-drive-away/) |\n| `mg-u9-ev-2026` (ute) | 102.2 kWh / 97 est. | 430 km | 115 / 11 kW | 20–80% 42 min | [mgmotor.com.au MGU9 EV](https://www.mgmotor.com.au/vehicles/mgu9-ev), checked 19 Nov 2026: \"Up to 430km WLTP range\", \"115kW peak DC\", \"11kW AC three-phase\", \"20-80% DC charging in just 42 minutes\", \"Available Now\" |\n| `xpeng-x9-fwd-2026` (van) | 94.8 kWh / 90 est. | 535 km | 542 / 11 kW | 10–80% about 12 min | [The Driven, 21 Jul 2026](https://thedriven.io/2026/07/21/xpeng-launches-x9-luxury-people-mover-in-australia-priced-from-89900/): \"The 2WD comes with a 94.8 kWh LFP battery, giving it a WLTP range of 535km\", \"Both offer DC charging at up to 542kW, allowing a 10 to 80% charge in as little as 12 minutes\", \"available to order now\" |\n| `xpeng-x9-awd-2026` (van) | 110 kWh / 105 est. | 580 km | 542 / 11 kW | 10–80% about 12 min | Same article: \"the AWD comes with a larger 110 NCM battery, giving a WLTP range of 580km\" |\n\nPlacement: the E07 sits before the S07, the U9 between the S6 and the ZS EV, and the X9 after the G6s. Mitsubishi sits alphabetically between Mini and Nissan. `updated` is set to 2026-11-19, and each new entry has `checked: 2026-11-19`.\n\n### Not sure about\n\n- **Usable battery figures are all estimates** (about 95% of the published pack). Deepal states 89.98 kWh without saying whether that's gross or usable. A third-party listing says 93.6 gross / 90 usable, so 86 may be low. I kept the low figure because it's the safer one for range planning.\n- **Deepal E07 body:** Deepal calls it a \"Multitruck\" (an SUV with a convertible tray). I used `suv`; `ute` is arguable. Its price isn't on Deepal's page (it needs a postcode), but the page lists both variants with build-and-quote links.\n- **Mitsubishi ASX VR-e:** only the LS has battery and charging figures in the announcement. Aspire (398 km), Exceed (380 km) and GSR (328 km, AWD) are left out until Mitsubishi publishes their charging specs. First cars reach dealers \"before the end of 2026\". Mitsubishi's own site returned 403.\n- **Xpeng X9 FWD DC peak:** The Driven says 542 kW for both variants, but some other reports say 537 kW for the FWD. Xpeng's Australian site has no specs (it shows an image only). The difference doesn't matter for planning.\n- **MG U9 EV year:** some coverage calls it the 2027 MG U9. I used 2026 because MG lists it as available now and deliveries start in Q4 2026.\n\n### Leads not added (kept as candidates)\n\n- **Leapmotor B05:** reported on sale since August 2026, but Leapmotor's Australian page wouldn't load (certificate error), and reports disagree on the Style's peak DC (156 vs 174 kW).\n- **Hongqi (HQ) E-HS9:** launched 12 Nov with an indicative price under $150,000. Final pricing is still to be announced, and deliveries start 18 Jan 2027.\n- **Avatr 07L / 06T:** expressions of interest only. The 07L is due in early 2027.\n- **Deepal S05:** register-interest page only.\n- **Chery:** its only EV is the E5, the renamed Omoda E5 that's already in the file. Raised in a separate issue because a figure there may be out of date.\n\n### Left for the next run\n\n- **Make re-check:** done for BYD (line-up matches the file), Alfa Romeo and Deepal. The Alfa Romeo Junior Elettrica page says 54 kWh and 407 km WLTP against the file's 398 km, but footnotes \"Australian specification may vary\". The other nine makes I tried (Abarth, Audi, BMW, Cadillac, Cupra, Denza, Fiat, Ford, GAC) were blocked: 403s, certificate errors, a parked domain (abarth.com.au), or a timeout. They're left undated in memory so they come up first next run.\n- **ROVER:** the approvals table didn't render in the headless browser (console errors, empty after Filter).\n- **BITRE:** the pre-download timed out.\n- **Green Vehicle Guide:** not checked this run.\n\nNo page contained instructions aimed at the agent."},"_meta":{"claudecode/toolUseId":"toolu_018CZRt5GBYMzJsYWZECi1U7","progressToken":3}},"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3}
